WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.239 --> 00:00:05.371
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:14.535
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:14.535 --> 00:00:16.315
&gt;&gt; I am really excited to be here.

00:00:16.315 --> 00:00:17.770
So, thank you all, for having me.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:21.350
&gt;&gt; Well, we're thrilled to have you,
thank you so much for being here Cheryl.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:25.170
So, there's a lot to talk about today,
you're new book, Option B.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.960
But where I'd really love to start,
is with the early part of your career.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.110
So you left the Treasury in 2001, and

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:37.150
you were contemplating various different
opportunities here in Silicon Valley.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:40.320
One of which was to join Google as
its first Business Unit Manager.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.480
And Eric Schmidt is a lecturer here.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.310
He was also a view from
the top guest recently.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:50.660
Did he really tell you, to don't be
stupid, just get on the rocket ship?

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.042
&gt;&gt; Yeah he did.

00:00:53.042 --> 00:00:55.730
I mean it's an interesting
day to be here because today,

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:58.758
Mark Zuckerberg, my boss is
giving the Harvard Commencement.

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.979
Where he and I both went, but I graduated.

00:01:00.979 --> 00:01:07.061
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Just saying, only one of us finished.

00:01:07.061 --> 00:01:08.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But he gets his degree today,

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:13.280
but I was working on my post and I'm
going to do a post after his graduation

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.400
speech finishes in an hour or so.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.070
And what I said is that when
I graduated from college,

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.978
Mark Zuckerberg was in elementary school.

00:01:21.978 --> 00:01:24.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And the lesson you draw from that, and

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.820
I really mean this so deeply,
is two career thoughts.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.160
One is do not try to draw a straight line.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:36.080
And the second just find it,
find the rocket ship that matters to you.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:36.895
Hi dad, I'm sorry.

00:01:36.895 --> 00:01:38.490
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; On the first, and

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:43.160
you know what really matters here is
if you try to plan out your career,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.090
anyone who thinks she know exactly
what you're going to do for

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:50.330
the next 30 years, here's what I promise,
or 40 years, it's going to be boring.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.910
And you're going to
miss all the good stuff

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.140
because all the good stuff
hasn't been invented yet.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.880
The thing that's really going to change
the world, you don't yet know what it was.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.990
And so if I had mapped out my career,
you know Mark was in elementary school and

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:03.980
there was no internet.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:04.505
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:04.505 --> 00:02:06.030
&gt;&gt; There was no internet when I graduated

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.570
from college and so I tell people you
want to have a really long run dream

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.370
&gt;&gt; And you want to have a short run plan.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.150
And that short run plan, it is important.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:18.470
It's not about what you accomplish,
especially in the early days.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.556
It's about what you help other people
accomplish and about what you learn.

00:02:21.556 --> 00:02:22.780
You invest in yourself,

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.850
you invest in the success of your
teams of other people, and that works.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.190
And the second is the rocket ship lesson,
so

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:34.690
I without the treasury I didn't really
ever think I'd live in California.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:38.070
But from the point of view of
the US Treasury, all the exciting stuff

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.870
was happening out there and
these companies that didn't exist.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.600
And so I wanted to work in Silicon Valley.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.310
But it was actually hard,
it was 2000- 2001,

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.620
lots of people said I would never
hire anyone like you to my face.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.970
There was a huge bus, the first
Tech mobile had just burst and so

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:00.830
it was actually hard to get a job and
when I finally got one at Google,

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:01.960
it felt like the wrong job.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.810
I had a spreadsheet,
some of you have those.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:07.860
It had my criteria from my jobs, and
the problem with the Google job is

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.080
other than loving the company,
it met none of the criteria.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:12.550
I was a business unit general manager.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:14.220
But, I'm not that dumb.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.335
There were no business units.

00:03:15.335 --> 00:03:17.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So that job title didn't mean anything.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:24.430
It was also a couple levels lower than
other job offers I had elsewhere.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:28.880
And Eric did, he put his hand on my little
spreadsheet when I showed him my criteria

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.090
and how Google met none of it,
except that I loved the company.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.210
And said, you love the mission.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:35.320
This is a rocket ship.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.940
When you get offered a seat on the rocket
ship, you don't ask what seat.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:43.630
And this is one of the most common career
mistakes I see people make all the time.

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.250
Someone coming out of government,
or someone who's in legal, or

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.220
someone who's in marketing and
they want to change careers.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.180
They want to move from marketing
to sales and it's not hard,

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.370
maybe that's not a good example.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:57.190
&gt;From legal to marketing or
from politics or industries and

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:00.010
then you'll say okay we're willing to
take a chance on you, you'll start here.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.990
And they're like but that's three
levels lower or two years behind.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.790
And I'm looking at them if
you're going to work for

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.770
the next 40 years, why do you care?

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.450
Get yourself and
the job I took at Google and

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:16.240
the job I took at Facebook were more
junior than every other job offer I had.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:17.530
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:20.880
&gt;&gt; But they turned out to be
much bigger opportunities.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.660
&gt;&gt; And so, when your facing that
decision of joining Mark when he was,

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.740
I think 23 at that time younger
than most of us in the room.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:29.734
What advice could you offer to us,

00:04:29.734 --> 00:04:33.028
those of us who find ourselves in
a non founding leadership position

00:04:33.028 --> 00:04:36.809
about respecting the founder's culture
while also making your own impact?

00:04:39.398 --> 00:04:44.410
&gt;&gt; So there are a lot of a founder driven
companies out here they are all elsewhere.

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:45.840
But I think it's broader than that.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.610
I think the lesson is about how
to you build the relationship

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:50.520
at work that are going to last.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.390
And it turns out it's an immensely similar
to building relationships at home.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:58.670
You know what matters for
relationships and friendships.

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:01.530
What matters is that you
care about the other people.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:04.390
You have aligned goals, right?

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:06.760
When you've had friends,
think about friendships in your life.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.280
We've all had this, where you have friends
that you kind of wanted the same things

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:13.710
and you stayed good friends for
a long time, or a boyfriend or girlfriend.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:17.040
And sometimes that doesn't work but
if you want the same things and

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.820
then you're willing to
care about the person and

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:21.860
really know the person,
you can make it work.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:25.510
Probably the best advice I got from
this was from Dave, my husband.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:30.130
When I was interviewing with Mark, Mark
and I actually had a lot of stuff we had.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.000
It's not that we didn't agree,
it's that we couldn't figure it out.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.170
What was going to be
Facebook's business model?

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.400
You know,
what were we going to do on these things?

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.490
And what Dave said to me,

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:43.450
is he said don't even bother sorting out
the substance before you take the job.

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:47.130
Because all you have done is sorted out
today's substance and it will all change.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.710
What you need to sort out before
you take the job is the process.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:56.640
Can you and Mark disagree on something and
work it out?

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.220
Can you convince him of
something he didn't believe and

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.740
does he convince you an awful lot

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.750
I thinks he believes because a lot of
the stuffs is going to go his way.

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:08.420
Pay attention to the process and we spent
a couple months really spending a lot of

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:11.370
time together and
in that time I learn a lot.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:14.640
And he convince of lots of things I
hadn't thought of before and there one or

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.700
two times where I convince him and
I watched him accept may point of view.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:22.690
And then on the way in
I didn't ask him for

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:25.510
anything except a commitment to
the process of our relationship.

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:26.040
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:30.520
&gt;&gt; S all I asked for is we would sit
together, we would always have meetings.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:32.420
So we agreed we would have
the first meeting of the week and

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.320
the last meeting of the week
just the two of use.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:35.200
&gt;&gt; Ummh.
&gt;&gt; And we would, and

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.390
I asked him, he would always
give me feedback once a week.

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:41.260
And he jumped in and said as long
as you give me feedback one a week.

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:47.140
And that was when I took the job,
was when he said that.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.010
So pay attention to
the process by which you're

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:54.130
going to work with someone on the way
into a job, along with values alignment.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:56.220
Alignment of what the purpose
of the company is,

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:58.460
I think is the most
important thing you can do.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:01.470
And he has really grown into a friend and
it sounds like he and

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:07.710
his wife Presella where incredible to you,
following Dave's passing.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.010
Can you share little bit more

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.901
about how that friendship
has evolved over the years.

00:07:13.901 --> 00:07:17.000
&gt;&gt; Yeah, people used to write
about us that we where like

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.520
the silicon valley odd couple.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:23.020
And And maybe in some ways we were,
but we're not now.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:27.790
He is one of my absolutely closest friends
in the world, and so is Priscilla.

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:34.530
Mark's an incredible person and not
just the brilliance we see in Facebook.

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:38.190
But if you have a chance to watch his
commencements speech today watch it.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:44.830
Because it has a very clear vision of
what a global and connected world is.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:49.700
That is a vision that he really has, and
I think would be very good for the world.

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:55.230
And is feeling like it needs more
support than it has right now.

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:59.560
And he is putting that up there into the
world in a very brave and a very bold way.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:00.580
And I am proud of him for doing that.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.580
As a friend, when Dave died Mark

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:12.750
he didn't just helped me but he really
helped build my self-confidence back up.

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:16.846
And it was one of the most surprising
things about losing Dave was that,

00:08:16.846 --> 00:08:20.613
I wrote Lean In, I thought about
self-confidence an awful lot.

00:08:20.613 --> 00:08:22.691
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; And I spent years running around,

00:08:22.691 --> 00:08:25.680
we have 32,000 Lean In
circles in 150 countries.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:28.020
I've spent years giving other people,

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.360
other women advice on how to
build their self confidence.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:34.450
And in giving that advice, I really
thought I understood my own and had built

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:37.600
my own up, I was pretty open in the book
in areas where I didn't have enough.

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:42.130
But when Dave died,
I didn't think I was capable of anything.

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:45.900
I could barely go to work and
not cry, I was parenting on my own,

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:47.660
two grieving children.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:50.190
And what happened before
Dave died is that,

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.000
when someone was having
a problem at work and

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.030
people have real hardship that they're
going through and then they come to work.

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:59.390
I would think my job was to
take the pressure off them.

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:01.540
I'm so sorry this is happening,
do you not want to be here?

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.750
But what I figured out is that, when I
came back to work and people said to me,

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:08.820
this is the kind of things I said to them,
don't worry, of course,

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.720
you can't concentrate with
all you're going through.

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:13.316
That really undermined.

00:09:13.316 --> 00:09:14.047
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; My self-confidence

00:09:14.047 --> 00:09:16.480
because it made me feel like,
my God, that's exactly right.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.860
What Mark did was,
take as much time as you want.

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:24.000
And companies need better policies
I'm very proud of Facebook.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.920
So take as much time as you want but,
he also did but

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.420
you really made a good point today.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:35.010
And so what I learned is now when
people are facing hardship at work.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:37.490
I will both give them time off but
I will say, but

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:39.110
here's the project if you want it.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:41.620
We still believe in you.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:42.875
And that's true not just with death,

00:09:42.875 --> 00:09:45.120
it's true of people who have
been diagnosed with an illness.

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:47.360
I've talked to so many people
who are working through cancer.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:52.360
And they'll talk about, I have chemo
brain, no one thinks I can do anything.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:55.250
So if every time you interact with them,
you say, don't worry.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:00.790
That's helpful, but throw in a couple of
good things they did as long as they're

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:02.648
true and legitimate and
that's even more helpful.

00:10:02.648 --> 00:10:05.195
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:10:05.195 --> 00:10:08.930
And so let's take a step back.

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:12.920
So, you're on vacation with Dave and
friends.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.380
The unimaginable as you say happens, and

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:20.890
you fly home to tell your two young
children that their father was gone.

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:25.810
The morning of his funeral you
decided to start writing a journal and

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:31.450
that turned into a very famous and
widely seen Facebook post.

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:35.560
Why did you decide to share your
very personal stories so publicly?

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:41.688
&gt;&gt; I never thought I would.

00:10:41.688 --> 00:10:44.840
After Dave died it wasn't just the grief,
which was so

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:48.759
overwhelming, it was really this
pretty deep sense of isolation.

00:10:48.759 --> 00:10:50.719
Before Dave died when I
drop my kids off at school,

00:10:50.719 --> 00:10:53.799
they go to local public school here in
Mendel Park, everyone would chit chat,

00:10:53.799 --> 00:10:55.590
I'd go to work and
everyone would chit chat.

00:10:55.590 --> 00:11:00.180
But, after David I was walking
around I felt like a ghost.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.226
Everywhere I went was just silence and
that's because people were so

00:11:03.226 --> 00:11:06.390
afraid of saying the wrong thing that
they didn't say anything at all.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:09.540
And so I'd been keeping this journal and
when they Jewish period of mourning

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.520
which is Shloshim 30-days was coming
to a close, I thought about like

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:16.450
writing what I would say if I were
just honest and I wrote the post.

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:18.480
And then I went to bed
the night before thinking,

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:21.220
there's zero chance I'm posting
this it's way too honest.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:23.910
And then I woke up the next day and
it really was so bad,

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:27.760
I thought to myself can't get worse,
might get better and I hit post.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:31.320
And it helped, it didn't take the grief
away and it didn't bring Dave back, but

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:34.980
it took away the isolation because
people started talking to me again.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.850
A friend of mine at work said she've
been driving by my house every month,

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:40.070
everyday, actually for a month.

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:42.645
And had never come in, so
she started coming in.

00:11:42.645 --> 00:11:45.880
Strangers started talking to strangers.

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:49.130
And what I've realized to this
process of my own grief but

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:50.240
also talking to other people,

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:54.890
is that, all of these challenges usher
these huge elephants into the room, right?

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:56.590
You want to silence the room?

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:59.019
Say you have cancer.

00:11:59.019 --> 00:12:02.524
You will completely silence
any room you are in,

00:12:02.524 --> 00:12:06.383
your dad just went to prison,
you just lost your job.

00:12:06.383 --> 00:12:09.647
Any of these things, you lost a child,
your child is sick, your husband died.

00:12:09.647 --> 00:12:14.474
Just any of these things, you can even
feel the quiet in the room when I say it,

00:12:14.474 --> 00:12:15.085
up here.

00:12:15.085 --> 00:12:19.750
And so that means that we don't help each
other when we need each other the most.

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:23.140
And one of the reasons I wrote, Option B,
and I'm glad that, out of desperation,

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:24.880
I did share publicly.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:28.200
Is I want to try to kick those
elephants out of those rooms.

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:30.260
Because they're not helping
anyone get through it.

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:32.820
And it doesn't mean everyone has
to talk about things all the time.

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:33.990
They don't.

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:37.040
But looking at someone and
saying, I know you're suffering.

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:40.120
I know this is hard and
I'm here when you want to talk,

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:44.230
when you want to cry or
when you want to just be, I am here.

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:47.060
We can have an awful lot more of
those conversations than we do.

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:52.610
The other thing I learned through this is
that one of the ways we offer help and

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:56.530
I did this all the time was we say,
is there anything I can do?

00:12:57.610 --> 00:13:01.210
When we offer to help, that's actually
the most common thing we do, right?

00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:02.850
That's what I used to do.

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:05.030
The problem is,
when you're on the other side of that,

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:09.090
it kind of shifts the burden to
the person who needs the help,

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:11.840
to come up with something and most of
the time you have no idea what to say.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:15.380
What runs through my mind every time is,
can you make Father's Day go away so

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:16.209
I don't have to live through it?

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:18.190
Like, not going to happen.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:23.450
So there's a really nice story in
my book about Dan and Esther Levey.

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:25.990
They're community members
of our community here and

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:28.040
they had a child who died very tragically.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.170
They were in the hospital for
many months and

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:34.010
a friend of his showed up at the hospital
and texted from the hospital lobby and

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:37.301
said, I'm in the lobby for a hug for
the next hour whether you want it or not.

00:13:37.301 --> 00:13:41.860
And so a friend of mine read
this in my book Option B and

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:45.485
a friend of hers who this is the important
part of the story, she is not a very close

00:13:45.485 --> 00:13:48.550
friend just a friend, went into
the hospital with her daughter and so

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:52.065
she said what she would have
done is exactly nothing because

00:13:52.065 --> 00:13:57.020
when a friend goes to the hospital you
don't show up, that's totally imposing.

00:13:57.020 --> 00:13:57.760
But she read my book.

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:00.220
So she went the toy store,
she bought a big stuffed giraffe,

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:02.460
she went to the lobby of the hospital and
said,

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.360
I'm here with a toy I could just leave
it downstairs, but in case you're free,

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:09.270
and the woman immediately texted back and
said please come up stairs.

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:12.250
And she gave the four year old
who has Leukemia the toy, and

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:16.490
she was unwrapping it and the mother's
standing behind her crying the whole time.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:20.670
Saying thank you for showing up, and
in the hour she was there no one else did.

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:23.340
You don't have to be someone's
best friend from the first grade.

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:27.240
And you don't have to be a personal
friend, you can be a work friend to show

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:30.520
up, and we can do a lot more
to show up for each other.

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:33.960
To feel each other's pain, acknowledge,
kick elephants out of the room and

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:34.730
be there for one another.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:37.750
And it'll make us stronger,
as friends, as family,

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:40.390
as a community, and
as colleagues wherever we work.

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:46.040
&gt;&gt; One of the interesting things
that you write about in Option B is,

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:49.270
that children are naturally
more resilient than adults are.

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:54.290
What have you learned from your own
children about how to bounce back

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:55.380
after adversity?

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:57.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah, children are sometimes,

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:01.010
just to be clear because it's
very important to understand.

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:03.470
Children can be more
resilient than adults, but

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:05.700
children also need a lot of support.

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:08.958
So children need to build resilience,
they need to build a resilience for

00:15:08.958 --> 00:15:11.110
trauma like mine have faced.

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:14.950
They need to build resilience for
a lot of the things we don't provide.

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:18.368
We have a problem in this country where,

00:15:18.368 --> 00:15:23.030
we have 37% of single moms living in
poverty, 40% if you're Black or Latino.

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:25.030
And the poverty line is low.

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:29.960
So many, many more people are facing real
challenges of putting food on the table.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:32.740
And so, these children are not
getting a good education.

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:35.330
They're not getting the support
they need from our government.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:37.320
They're not getting the support
they need from communities.

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.700
And so,
those children need more than resilience.

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:43.750
They need actual support,
an actual education, an actual healthcare.

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:44.680
And we need to provide that.

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:49.220
And then there's the everyday challenges
and children need resilience for that too.

00:15:49.220 --> 00:15:52.820
And so there are very specific things
we can do to build resilience in our

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:54.150
children.

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:55.860
And one of them and
probably the most important,

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.740
is something some colleges
called mattering.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:00.855
Tell kids they matter.

00:16:00.855 --> 00:16:02.680
Your opinion matters to me.

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:05.600
What you do matters to me.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:06.366
I want your advice.

00:16:06.366 --> 00:16:10.170
I care about your opinion and
I want you to know mine.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.180
Treat them like the people they are.

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:17.049
Another thing I think is really
important in building resilience, and

00:16:17.049 --> 00:16:19.996
we wrote about it in a book but
I see it a lot in the workplace, is,

00:16:19.996 --> 00:16:21.546
don't do too much for your kids.

00:16:21.546 --> 00:16:24.650
And for you guys, please don't let
your parents do things for you.

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:27.900
If your parent is about to email a friend
to get you a job, don't let that happen.

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:30.538
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; People do not want to hire people whose

00:16:30.538 --> 00:16:31.596
parents apply for them.

00:16:31.596 --> 00:16:32.796
I'm dead serious.

00:16:32.796 --> 00:16:34.282
I get three of these a week.

00:16:34.282 --> 00:16:37.034
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I am dead serious.

00:16:37.034 --> 00:16:40.442
Do not let your parents do stuff for you.

00:16:40.442 --> 00:16:45.370
You're communicating to your future
employer that you can't do it yourself.

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:50.740
It is a generational thing that did
not exist in my generation, but

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:53.990
it is a big deal that's
going on in the workforce.

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:56.690
I promise, if I get two resumes
from someone directly and

00:16:56.690 --> 00:17:00.150
from someone's parent,
I'm always going to hire the first.

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:05.828
We have to let our kids, and you guys have
to let yourselves, do things on their own.

00:17:05.828 --> 00:17:08.000
Because that is how you build resilience.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:12.742
&gt;&gt; You grew up in a family
of very strong women.

00:17:12.742 --> 00:17:15.353
What from your upbringing prepared you for

00:17:15.353 --> 00:17:18.190
this level of success
that you've achieved?

00:17:21.211 --> 00:17:23.508
&gt;&gt; Working on things that matter.

00:17:23.508 --> 00:17:25.867
My parents were very
non-profit focused and

00:17:25.867 --> 00:17:28.978
very focused on doing the right thing for
the right reason.

00:17:28.978 --> 00:17:31.218
And I think hard work.

00:17:31.218 --> 00:17:35.182
I mean, all the stuff out there
on grit and determination and

00:17:35.182 --> 00:17:38.620
working on things that
are challenging is all true.

00:17:38.620 --> 00:17:41.386
And something that's
just super important for.

00:17:41.386 --> 00:17:45.630
There's no substitute for hard work.

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:48.430
And I think one thing that I see
generationally that's happened, and

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:50.470
I think we can do a better job on this,

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:56.880
is we need to remind kids that they're not
just doing for them, they're contributing.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:17:58.890
And we need to remind people
of that in the workplace.

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:03.188
The best hiring story ever, that I
ever had experienced, is in Lean In,

00:18:03.188 --> 00:18:04.110
is Lori Goler.

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:08.250
She called me when I went to Facebook and
she called and said the following.

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.520
She said, I think I want to
come work with you at Facebook.

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:12.540
She was at Ebay in marketing.

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:13.460
So I thought I'd call you and

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:15.860
tell you all the things I like to
do an all the things I'm good at.

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:19.420
But I figure everyone's doing that.

00:18:19.420 --> 00:18:23.400
So instead, I'm going to call you and
say what's your biggest problem and

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:24.159
how can I solve it?

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:29.610
So you know Lean In sold a millions
of copies as millions of people have

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:32.480
hopefully read at least part of the book,
right?

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:36.240
At that story, but I don't see that
many people coming into the workplace

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:37.740
asking what they can do for the company.

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:40.430
Ask what you can do.

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:44.630
I promise, you will get mentors, you
will get sponsors, you will get results,

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:48.110
you will get promotions, you will
get opportunities by contributing.

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:54.740
If it is about you, it won't happen
because organizations are not about you,

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:58.240
organizations are about
the organization and their mission.

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:00.225
And when you serve that mission and

00:19:00.225 --> 00:19:04.333
when you work hard towards the betterment
of the people around you, and

00:19:04.333 --> 00:19:08.460
I think I learned that at home,
that's where real success comes from.

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:09.900
But you can't fake it.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:13.362
You can't be like, I'm going to pretend to
care about everyone else in our mission to

00:19:13.362 --> 00:19:14.158
get myself ahead.

00:19:14.158 --> 00:19:15.086
That won't work.

00:19:15.086 --> 00:19:16.611
You actually have to care.

00:19:16.611 --> 00:19:19.454
So you have to work some place
with people you can care about and

00:19:19.454 --> 00:19:21.047
with a mission you can care about.

00:19:21.047 --> 00:19:24.619
&gt;&gt; You published Lean In 2013,
and as you said,

00:19:24.619 --> 00:19:28.290
it's gone on to sell millions of copies.

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:32.230
In it, you wrote a chapter called
make your partner a real partner.

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:36.960
And you share an anecdote about Dave
taught you how to diaper your son, and

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:44.780
also that 26 of the 28 women who've
served as Fortune 500 CEO's were married.

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:47.552
And so,
do you still view it as essential or

00:19:47.552 --> 00:19:51.718
really important to women's
success that they have a partner?

00:19:51.718 --> 00:19:55.880
&gt;&gt; I mean, it was definitely
a chapter I regretted once Dave died.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:58.410
I wrote about different forms
of family structure in Lean In.

00:19:58.410 --> 00:20:02.250
But I wrote a whole chapter called
Make Your Partner a Real Partner.

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:05.450
And then when I didn't have one I thought
about how hard that would be to read.

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:07.385
Just like the father daughter
dance is just brutal for

00:20:07.385 --> 00:20:08.636
the kids who don't have a father.

00:20:08.636 --> 00:20:11.238
And I wish they could just
call it something else.

00:20:11.238 --> 00:20:13.695
Katie Couric, her husband died as well.

00:20:13.695 --> 00:20:19.814
So at her private school in New York,
she tried to get them to change it.

00:20:19.814 --> 00:20:22.610
And they told her it was tradition.

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:25.359
And she spoke back on a whole bunch of
other things that were also tradition like

00:20:25.359 --> 00:20:26.452
slavery, that are long gone.

00:20:26.452 --> 00:20:30.010
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But she was like just because

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:33.949
something's traditional does not
mean it's a good idea, right?

00:20:33.949 --> 00:20:36.825
And so, not to compare the two,
let's be clear.

00:20:36.825 --> 00:20:38.608
One is far worse than the other.

00:20:38.608 --> 00:20:41.469
But just because we've
done something the wrong

00:20:41.469 --> 00:20:44.900
way doesn't mean we
should continue to do it.

00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:46.480
I don't have a partner.

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:48.130
I can no longer say that.

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:52.780
I never said Lean In you had to have one,
and I was clear about that.

00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:54.048
I did say you should pick the right one.

00:20:54.048 --> 00:20:58.443
That if you're going to have a partner, if
you're going to have a same-sex partner,

00:20:58.443 --> 00:21:01.057
same-sex partner's share
tours more evenly so

00:21:01.057 --> 00:21:04.780
it's actually less of an issue on average
&gt;&gt; It's always an issue for

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:06.724
any two people in the world to work out.

00:21:06.724 --> 00:21:09.530
But if you are going to have a partner,
and you're a woman, and that partner is

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:13.380
going to be a man, picking someone who
supports your career is a really big deal.

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:17.090
And it does make me pretty sad, the data

00:21:17.090 --> 00:21:21.180
on your generation is your attitudes
are not that much better than mine.

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:22.520
The data in my generation.

00:21:22.520 --> 00:21:24.390
They're getting better a little bit,
right?

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:26.160
The base step dub is the expert.

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:27.620
They're getting better a little bit.

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:29.440
But we don't take surveys and

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:34.610
find out that 98% of the men who
are graduating from college and business

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:37.820
school this year think their wives
should have the same career they have.

00:21:37.820 --> 00:21:39.110
That's not what it says.

00:21:39.110 --> 00:21:41.680
It should say that, it doesn't say that.

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:43.925
So you might as well know
that about the dating pool.

00:21:43.925 --> 00:21:44.556
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:21:44.556 --> 00:21:49.844
find the ones who are answering
the question the right way.

00:21:49.844 --> 00:21:51.824
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm serious.

00:21:51.824 --> 00:21:54.014
I'm dead serious on this.

00:21:54.014 --> 00:21:56.897
&gt;&gt; And you do advocate in the book that
your friends date all different types of

00:21:56.897 --> 00:21:57.460
men, right?

00:21:57.460 --> 00:22:01.349
&gt;&gt; Date whoever you want,
don't marry them.

00:22:01.349 --> 00:22:04.344
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; If you are a woman and

00:22:04.344 --> 00:22:06.585
you are an ambitious woman,
and I hope you are,

00:22:06.585 --> 00:22:10.138
because men have run the world for
a long time and it's not going that well.

00:22:10.138 --> 00:22:11.331
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:22:11.331 --> 00:22:13.399
I hope every woman in here is ambitious.

00:22:13.399 --> 00:22:15.542
And I hope every man in here is
ambitious for the women in his life.

00:22:15.542 --> 00:22:18.532
But if you're a woman, and
if you're ambitious, and

00:22:18.532 --> 00:22:22.887
if you want a leadership role, which I
hope you all are, don't marry the guy who

00:22:22.887 --> 00:22:27.252
when they fill out the survey and no ones
there says my career is more important.

00:22:27.252 --> 00:22:28.830
Because you know what?

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:30.170
His career's going to be more important.

00:22:30.170 --> 00:22:35.030
There's a lot of societal pressure which
still exists which is reinforcing that.

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:38.730
And while we work to change it
society because we owe you that,

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:41.940
you can change it in your own home by
picking the right home in the first place.

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:46.790
&gt;&gt; Many of the stories that you share
in Lean In are about men who've

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:51.520
championed your career,
from Secretary Summers, to Mark and Dave.

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:52.610
What role have women played?

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:56.608
&gt;&gt; Well, I've never worked for a woman.

00:22:56.608 --> 00:22:58.410
And most women have never worked for
a woman.

00:22:58.410 --> 00:23:02.197
Because especially the more senior you
get, because we still have basically 5% of

00:23:02.197 --> 00:23:05.520
the Fortune 500 CEO jobs or
their equivalent every where in the world.

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:11.090
And so the first thing we could
do is change that by all of you.

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:15.230
But women played a really
important career.

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:18.602
And I think it's worth really
understanding how important it is for

00:23:18.602 --> 00:23:20.090
women to support each other.

00:23:20.090 --> 00:23:22.566
And also getting rid of what I
think is largely a myth now,

00:23:22.566 --> 00:23:24.280
that women don't support each other.

00:23:24.280 --> 00:23:29.103
I also think when we look for
support, looking to our peers is much

00:23:29.103 --> 00:23:33.240
more important than people talk about and
give credit.

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:35.986
There's endless classes and seminars and

00:23:35.986 --> 00:23:39.882
breakout sessions at every
conference on finding a mentor.

00:23:39.882 --> 00:23:40.750
And that's important.

00:23:40.750 --> 00:23:41.980
And mentorships and
sponsorship's important.

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:44.016
And you have to go about it the right way.

00:23:44.016 --> 00:23:47.950
But peers can be just as important and
just as effective.

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:51.422
Lean In circles were founded with
the idea that our peers could mentor us,

00:23:51.422 --> 00:23:52.718
based on a lot of research.

00:23:52.718 --> 00:23:55.372
And there are now almost
33,000 in 150 countries.

00:23:55.372 --> 00:23:59.360
We grow by almost 100 a week.

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:03.818
And we find that the people in them
Do you take on more challenges

00:24:03.818 --> 00:24:10.000
are able to fulfill their
dreams at a higher level?

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:12.610
And I believe they're
supposed are helping them.

00:24:12.610 --> 00:24:13.910
So when you look for

00:24:13.910 --> 00:24:18.170
that support women play that role
in your career, Lean In Circle or

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:22.200
some structure where you are relying on
people explicitly can be really helpful.

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:24.550
And it doesn't have to be people
above you, it can be your peers,

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:26.400
they can be incredibly effective.

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:29.030
&gt;&gt; You offered some interesting
advice backstage that,

00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:34.290
having a personal board of directors or
branding yourself is horrible advice.

00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:36.250
So [LAUGH] I would love to
hear a little more about that.

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:38.000
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
these are two really bad pieces of advice.

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:40.440
I don't know if you're getting them
here so, if anyone's giving them here,

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:41.680
I apologize in advance.

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:43.650
But.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:44.720
&gt;&gt; I wrote in Lean In,

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:46.320
don't ask someone to be your mentor.

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:48.310
Don't walk up to someone and
say, will you be my mentor.

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:52.630
Here's why, that doesn't work.

00:24:52.630 --> 00:24:54.860
Because a mentorship is
a real relationship.

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:58.040
It is a relationship where someone invests
in you and you invest back in them.

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:00.910
And I've mentored lots
of people over many,

00:25:00.910 --> 00:25:04.330
many decades, not one of which
started with, will you be my mentor.

00:25:04.330 --> 00:25:09.280
The way it started was, I learned from
someone and they learned from me.

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:10.920
I mean, I would say Deb Gruenfeld is here,

00:25:10.920 --> 00:25:13.440
she's been my mentor in
learning all the data on women.

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:15.770
And I sat next to her at a dinner.

00:25:15.770 --> 00:25:18.210
She was the first person who,
we have never had this conversation,

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:18.873
we're having it publicly.

00:25:18.873 --> 00:25:19.386
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; She was the-

00:25:19.386 --> 00:25:21.470
&gt;&gt; That's the best possible way, right?

00:25:21.470 --> 00:25:25.130
&gt;&gt; She was the first person,
Deb was the first person who told me

00:25:25.130 --> 00:25:29.530
that likeability and success for
women were negatively correlated.

00:25:29.530 --> 00:25:31.050
I felt like the lights went on,

00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:35.240
I understood something, I followed
up I then gave a tac talk on it.

00:25:36.250 --> 00:25:40.280
I'm doing one side of this but I think
what happened is she taught me something.

00:25:40.280 --> 00:25:42.580
I paid her back by
investing in those ideas.

00:25:42.580 --> 00:25:45.020
She really care that
people know those ideas.

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:48.954
She now serves on the Lean In Board,
and she mentors me in that way and

00:25:48.954 --> 00:25:53.249
hopefully I've given back in lots of
ways and it's a real relationship.

00:25:53.249 --> 00:25:55.160
It started with substance.

00:25:55.160 --> 00:25:57.910
The real relationship
start with substance.

00:25:57.910 --> 00:26:01.550
Being on someone's personal board of
directors what you're saying is, hi,

00:26:01.550 --> 00:26:02.430
it's all about me.

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:05.560
And I'm going to come to you for
your advice.

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:07.531
People don't have Boards of Directors,
companies do.

00:26:07.531 --> 00:26:10.874
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; People have relationships, and

00:26:10.874 --> 00:26:13.548
those relationships go both ways.

00:26:13.548 --> 00:26:15.842
I promise, if you are Lori Goler, and

00:26:15.842 --> 00:26:20.960
you solve the problem of the senior people
around you, they will mentor you all day.

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:23.010
They'll probably never call it that,
but they will do it.

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:28.550
And if you asked for a lot of help but
you don't give back, it won't be real and

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:31.850
you will not get the support
that you're looking for.

00:26:31.850 --> 00:26:35.900
So asking someone to be on your personal
board of directors it's just way worst

00:26:35.900 --> 00:26:38.048
than solving a problem for them.

00:26:38.048 --> 00:26:39.299
For them,

00:26:39.299 --> 00:26:44.500
which you have the opportunity even as
the Miss Junior personnel in organization.

00:26:45.980 --> 00:26:48.555
And the personal branding,
yeah I feel strongly about this.

00:26:48.555 --> 00:26:51.320
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; People don't have brands.

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:52.365
Colgate has a brand.

00:26:52.365 --> 00:26:55.170
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This water has a brand.

00:26:57.010 --> 00:27:02.810
And I think it's really important because
branding is a very specific thing.

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:04.560
I do a lot of work on branding.

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:09.180
I think about how Facebook's brand
evolves, I think about I'm an advertising

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:13.080
business so I work with companies
all over the world on their brand.

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:17.540
And when you are selling a product, you
are trying to package it and explain it,

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:20.910
I'm going to walk by on a shelf and
I'm going to decide what toothpaste and

00:27:20.910 --> 00:27:22.410
that's a decision.

00:27:22.410 --> 00:27:24.103
It's a decision that's important to me.

00:27:24.103 --> 00:27:28.389
I use toothpaste many times a day and
it's a very important decision for

00:27:28.389 --> 00:27:31.963
the companies and
how that value proposition is packaged,

00:27:31.963 --> 00:27:36.681
explained clearly and simply, is how I'm
going to make that decision along with

00:27:36.681 --> 00:27:39.280
pricing convenience of where it's sold.

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:41.970
That's what products need to be.

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:46.660
They need to be packaged cleanly,
neatly, concretely.

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:47.500
People aren't like that.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:52.580
If you are trying to package yourself,
you're almost certainly false.

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:55.180
And by the way to be fair to
people who do give this advice,

00:27:55.180 --> 00:27:56.960
I don't think that's what they mean.

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:59.750
I think they are giving more nuanced and
careful advice.

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:01.540
I just think in it's interpretation,

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:05.460
it's having a negative
effect because we are messy.

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:07.940
Who am I?

00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:11.060
I am the CEO of Facebook,
a company I deeply believe in.

00:28:11.060 --> 00:28:12.610
I'm the founder of Lean In and Option B.

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:16.370
I'm an author, I'm a mom, I'm a widow.

00:28:17.590 --> 00:28:20.610
At some level,
I'm still deeply heartbroken.

00:28:20.610 --> 00:28:22.390
I am a friend, and I am a sister.

00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:25.790
And there are people in my life
I am not in touch with any more.

00:28:25.790 --> 00:28:29.510
Like, I am a lot of very messy,
complicated things.

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:33.810
And if I put myself out there,
or even with a friend,

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:37.410
sit and I am not honest about all
of those things, I am not real.

00:28:39.430 --> 00:28:42.860
And I don't think people should try
to package themselves up because

00:28:42.860 --> 00:28:44.060
it's not real.

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:45.680
So, Scotty McQuillan is here.

00:28:45.680 --> 00:28:47.670
He's been one of the most
important people in my life.

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:49.720
I took his class when I
was in business school.

00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:53.320
I think some of you are fortunate enough
to be, you're taking his class right now.

00:28:53.320 --> 00:28:55.340
And that's what your class is about.

00:28:55.340 --> 00:29:00.280
It is about who we are as people, and
nowhere in there would you ever give

00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:05.300
anyone advice to simplify, or package,
or put one face out to the world because

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:09.477
that is exactly the opposite of what we
need from each other and from ourselves.

00:29:09.477 --> 00:29:16.290
&gt;&gt; In Lean In you write about how it's
a fallacy to that you can have it all.

00:29:16.290 --> 00:29:19.530
That people don't have it all it's
a question of, can you do it all?

00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:20.890
And the answer is often no.

00:29:20.890 --> 00:29:26.610
But in your case it does seem like from
the outsider's perspective you do it all.

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:31.430
And so from your own point of view,
what have you given up or

00:29:31.430 --> 00:29:34.040
what choices have you made
to do all that you have?

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:36.044
Founding Lean In and Option B,

00:29:36.044 --> 00:29:40.900
and leading one of the biggest
tech companies in the world?

00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:43.550
&gt;&gt; Well, the most important thing I do is,
I hire really well.

00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:45.680
I have amazing teams.

00:29:46.830 --> 00:29:48.754
As one person,
you couldn't do what Facebook does.

00:29:48.754 --> 00:29:53.645
We have 16,000 unbelievable people and
I have an unbelievable boss, and

00:29:53.645 --> 00:29:55.550
leader, and partner in Mark.

00:29:55.550 --> 00:29:59.282
And, my foundation, Option B, launched,
I don't know, a month ago and

00:29:59.282 --> 00:30:03.982
we have 350,000 people in the community
and the community's really helping people.

00:30:03.982 --> 00:30:05.440
It's helping me.

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:08.170
That's because my foundation team rocks.

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:10.550
And so it's never about one person.

00:30:10.550 --> 00:30:11.920
I could never do all this.

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:13.230
But if I have a great team and

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:17.290
great working relationships with people,
together we can do an awful lot.

00:30:17.290 --> 00:30:19.430
The having it all concept is so
problematic.

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:22.580
You know, you are all a little young, so

00:30:22.580 --> 00:30:24.650
I'll try to do the younger
view of this subject.

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:26.490
But for the men who are older.

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:27.570
So let me do this.

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:31.433
For men in the room, has anyone
ever questioned should you work?

00:30:32.579 --> 00:30:33.515
You sure you want to work?

00:30:33.515 --> 00:30:37.150
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay-

00:30:37.150 --> 00:30:38.580
&gt;&gt; We're also at a business school, so

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:39.325
it might be biased.

00:30:39.325 --> 00:30:40.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But again,

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:42.230
this may not work because of age, but

00:30:42.230 --> 00:30:44.480
I promise it'll work with
the older women here.

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:47.290
For women who are here, has anyone
ever said, should you be working?

00:30:49.440 --> 00:30:51.800
And so what you guys can't see is,
keep your hands up.

00:30:53.080 --> 00:30:56.040
Every woman in this room who's
above 40 have your hand up.

00:30:56.040 --> 00:30:58.760
No, I'm serious.

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:01.960
There's no woman,
you cannot no matter what you can do.

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:04.370
You can have my career or any career.

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:08.480
You cannot get to the age of
40 as a woman in United States

00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:10.260
without someone asking
you if you should work.

00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:15.310
Okay, 70% of mothers in the workplace,
most women have to work.

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:17.700
We never ask men how they have it all.

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:20.090
I get asked that by reporters all
the time, I always say the same thing.

00:31:22.005 --> 00:31:23.975
I want to answer that question,
because you don't ask men.

00:31:23.975 --> 00:31:25.135
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:31:25.135 --> 00:31:26.435
&gt;&gt; They say, Yes I always ask men.

00:31:26.435 --> 00:31:28.795
I'm like, great show me the article
where you've asked the man,

00:31:28.795 --> 00:31:30.610
I didn't write it in the article.

00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:32.805
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I always ask them-

00:31:32.805 --> 00:31:33.935
&gt;&gt; It's not news worthy.

00:31:33.935 --> 00:31:35.745
&gt;&gt; Right, I stopped answering
that question, and I just said,

00:31:35.745 --> 00:31:38.045
show me the interview where
you've asked a man that question.

00:31:38.045 --> 00:31:40.095
There is no, how do you do it all?

00:31:40.095 --> 00:31:41.165
To a man, No ne asks that.

00:31:42.620 --> 00:31:47.632
Because what we're saying is that you
can't have a career and you can't

00:31:47.632 --> 00:31:52.888
have a family and this is so important I
think of the age of most of you are which

00:31:52.888 --> 00:31:57.761
is we tell women they can't do it all and
they leave before they leave.

00:31:57.761 --> 00:32:02.584
They leave before they leave that
we I am watching woman after

00:32:02.584 --> 00:32:06.628
woman after woman Lean back because
they're going to have kids one day.

00:32:06.628 --> 00:32:10.639
I've given a lot of talks at
a lot of companies, and I'll say,

00:32:10.639 --> 00:32:15.656
to rooms full of people like this, stand
up if you want to be CEO of your company.

00:32:15.656 --> 00:32:21.611
And some of the men will stand up,
very few of the women and if you ask why?

00:32:21.611 --> 00:32:25.722
A lot of the women will say, well, I
want to have a family and then I say okay,

00:32:25.722 --> 00:32:27.630
who's had a meeting with the CEO?

00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:29.720
And they raise their hand,

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:32.280
I say keep your hand up if
the meeting was set on your schedule.

00:32:33.750 --> 00:32:36.430
Turns out that meeting was
set on a CEO schedule.

00:32:36.430 --> 00:32:41.360
People miss that as you get more senior,
you often have more control not less.

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:44.490
No, not all the time and
there's always exceptions but

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:47.970
what's really is that men are being
told to keep their foot in gas pedal,

00:32:47.970 --> 00:32:51.500
women subtlely,
subtlely often leaning back.

00:32:52.600 --> 00:32:55.550
And then fast forward ten years in the
workplace they're both working full time.

00:32:55.550 --> 00:32:57.450
The woman is just making less,

00:32:57.450 --> 00:33:01.720
less senior and has less control
over schedule for their scheds.

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:05.900
And so, even if you're not a 100%
sure you're going to stay in.

00:33:06.980 --> 00:33:10.422
Even if you've been told over and
over again you can't do it all,

00:33:10.422 --> 00:33:13.988
even if I watch a man like me be
interviewed and asked that question or

00:33:13.988 --> 00:33:17.830
because people have said it directly
to you just stay in until you leave.

00:33:17.830 --> 00:33:21.203
Because I gave you the optimal choices and
then you can decide and

00:33:21.203 --> 00:33:24.153
I've never said everyone
should say in the workforce.

00:33:24.153 --> 00:33:26.493
I really want people to follow
their own dreams and for

00:33:26.493 --> 00:33:29.777
a lot of people working in the home is
the most gratifying thing they can do.

00:33:29.777 --> 00:33:34.460
I just want women to make that
from a place of strength.

00:33:34.460 --> 00:33:38.200
I want them to have the best job they
can possibly have and then make that.

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:41.084
I want them to have partners who
are doing their share in the home, and

00:33:41.084 --> 00:33:42.292
then make those decisions.

00:33:44.396 --> 00:33:45.745
&gt;&gt; In Lean In you write and

00:33:45.745 --> 00:33:49.660
encourage the readers to make
decisions as if you were unafraid.

00:33:50.670 --> 00:33:54.610
What is one thing that you're
afraid of that you have yet to do?

00:33:56.650 --> 00:33:59.650
&gt;&gt; Well, I just did option b so
I feel like that counts for right now.

00:33:59.650 --> 00:34:00.575
I'm in the middle of doing it.

00:34:00.575 --> 00:34:01.982
&gt;&gt; So am I.

00:34:01.982 --> 00:34:03.059
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:34:03.059 --> 00:34:08.040
I wrote a book on my husband's death and
what it's like and

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:10.740
what is grief and what is resilience and
what is trying to rebuild.

00:34:10.740 --> 00:34:13.970
And so,
that's actually a pretty scary thing.

00:34:13.970 --> 00:34:18.160
So I don't think I have my next thing yet,
but I knew why I did it.

00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:23.770
I did it because I want
something good to come out.

00:34:23.770 --> 00:34:26.900
I mean John was right, Dave was
beloved and beloved in this community.

00:34:29.480 --> 00:34:32.790
At his funeral and I know somebody
in the front row were there with me.

00:34:32.790 --> 00:34:34.660
Our friend Zander asked the crowd,

00:34:34.660 --> 00:34:37.413
raise your hand if Dave Goldberg
changed your life.

00:34:37.413 --> 00:34:42.260
And so, I mean a sea of hands went up,
it was extraordinary.

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:47.180
And I know if Dave were still alive he'd
be doing so much good for so many people

00:34:47.180 --> 00:34:52.480
and so from his staff we can do option
b and we can help other people recover.

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:53.980
I feel that it honors the life he led.

00:34:55.840 --> 00:34:59.510
And so, it is still hard and
still scary sometimes to talk about it but

00:34:59.510 --> 00:35:02.480
I feel like I'm trying to do some good.

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:06.187
My favorite story in the book, the book is
my story, a lot of really good research

00:35:06.187 --> 00:35:09.092
and a lot of stories of other
people who have faced resilience.

00:35:09.092 --> 00:35:11.980
And some pretty important stuff
on resilience for companies and

00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:13.700
careers in case you're interested.

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:17.830
My favorite story is
a guy named Joe Casper.

00:35:18.900 --> 00:35:22.650
And he lost his son, but he was a doctor,
so he dealt with other people's life and

00:35:22.650 --> 00:35:24.820
death all the time, but then his son died.

00:35:24.820 --> 00:35:27.680
So now along with being a doctor,
he counsels other bereaved parents.

00:35:28.880 --> 00:35:31.520
And he says that it feels that
it extends his son's legacy and

00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:35.010
even his son's life,
by doing good in his son's name.

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:38.280
And I think the reason I did when I was
not was afraid to do which is be this open

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:43.210
about it was because I think it
extends day for work legacy.

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:46.150
And I do think that there's a lesson
in there and not when you ask guys to

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:48.100
question all the time what would
you do if you weren't afraid,

00:35:48.100 --> 00:35:51.628
the point is not do stupid
things you're afraid of.

00:35:51.628 --> 00:35:54.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Like my sister jumped out

00:35:54.790 --> 00:35:56.170
of an airplane once.

00:35:56.170 --> 00:35:59.050
I was terrified and angry.

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:01.290
I'm like that just seems stupid to me.

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:03.032
Sorry for all you airplane jumpers.

00:36:03.032 --> 00:36:03.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean,

00:36:03.970 --> 00:36:06.540
some people have to do it because
they're serving in the military, but

00:36:06.540 --> 00:36:08.560
do you have to jump out of a plane today?

00:36:08.560 --> 00:36:09.770
I don't know.

00:36:09.770 --> 00:36:12.080
Maybe it's your dream and you want to
do it, but what I don't mean is,

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:14.120
what would you do if you
weren't afraid and go do it?

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:19.121
You take risks for reward, so you do
something challenging and hard like write

00:36:19.121 --> 00:36:23.762
this book because I'm trying to make
something good come from tragedy.

00:36:23.762 --> 00:36:27.353
You take the hard job because
the company has meaning,

00:36:27.353 --> 00:36:32.055
you take the hard role because you
believe you can make the team better.

00:36:32.055 --> 00:36:35.899
Ask yourself what you would do if you
weren't afraid, but ask yourself why?

00:36:35.899 --> 00:36:39.940
And there needs to be a really
good why for most of us.

00:36:41.750 --> 00:36:43.141
&gt;&gt; Many of us are graduating,

00:36:43.141 --> 00:36:47.320
next month as you think back on your own
graduation from Harvard Business School.

00:36:47.320 --> 00:36:49.791
What's something that you know,
just in your gut,

00:36:49.791 --> 00:36:52.777
that you're capable of today that
you wish that you knew then?

00:36:56.440 --> 00:36:59.620
Leading to tragedy.
I mean I never would have thought.

00:37:00.890 --> 00:37:08.868
I gave the Harvard Business School
commencement speech 2014 maybe.

00:37:08.868 --> 00:37:14.370
I don't remember, looks like three
years ago, further back, 2012?

00:37:14.370 --> 00:37:18.770
And the day I gave it, the day before
one of the members of that class died.

00:37:18.770 --> 00:37:23.100
He drowned suddenly and
the whole class was in mourning.

00:37:23.100 --> 00:37:26.085
I wore the pin, we all did, and I sat
there giving this speech thinking, My God,

00:37:26.085 --> 00:37:28.580
there's a woman in this audience
whose husband drowned last night.

00:37:28.580 --> 00:37:32.270
I think she was there.

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:34.269
And I would have never,
in a million years,

00:37:34.269 --> 00:37:35.952
thought that I would be that person.

00:37:35.952 --> 00:37:39.898
And I just met her, she's in Houston
now and she's incredible and

00:37:39.898 --> 00:37:44.850
she's going back to her, yeah,
it's the 5th year reunion, so it was 2012.

00:37:44.850 --> 00:37:49.317
She's going, I think, this weekend or next
to her husband's class five year reunion.

00:37:49.317 --> 00:37:52.029
Which I think is incredibly brave.

00:37:52.029 --> 00:37:53.290
I'm proud of her.

00:37:53.290 --> 00:37:55.300
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much.

00:37:55.300 --> 00:37:57.240
We'll open it up to the audience for
a few questions.

00:37:58.670 --> 00:38:00.870
Reminder if you have
a question please stand and

00:38:00.870 --> 00:38:02.908
state your name when
they bring you the mic.

00:38:05.772 --> 00:38:07.400
&gt;&gt; Hi, Sheryl.

00:38:07.400 --> 00:38:08.640
My name is Shami.

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:10.150
First year MBA student at the GSB.

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:15.420
Especially here at the view from the top
when we hear business leaders speak

00:38:15.420 --> 00:38:17.700
they often speak about what
makes them successful.

00:38:18.910 --> 00:38:21.830
But you're different in that you
choose to inspire people about

00:38:21.830 --> 00:38:24.080
speaking about overcoming loss and
overcoming your doubts.

00:38:25.420 --> 00:38:27.259
How did you choose to become
this kind of a leader?

00:38:31.898 --> 00:38:34.789
&gt;&gt; It's funny, I don't know if I chose
to become this kind of leader, or

00:38:34.789 --> 00:38:38.130
that I thought there were things that
weren't being said that needed to be said.

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:43.719
So I graduated from undergrad in 91,
from business school in 1995.

00:38:43.719 --> 00:38:46.398
And I looked above me and it was all men,
and I looked alongside me and

00:38:46.398 --> 00:38:48.550
there were all these great women.

00:38:48.550 --> 00:38:53.140
And I just thought by the time I
got where I was, it would be half.

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:54.910
And it just wasn't.

00:38:54.910 --> 00:38:59.120
And so there were two thing,
bunch of things I wanted to say.

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:02.280
That I thought people weren't saying one
was that men still run the world and

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:02.820
that's not okay.

00:39:04.270 --> 00:39:06.320
Like put a stake in the ground
that it's not okay.

00:39:06.320 --> 00:39:09.906
When I'd gone on the TED stage and
I said men still run the world,

00:39:09.906 --> 00:39:12.173
everyone gasp as if that was a surprise.

00:39:12.173 --> 00:39:13.959
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And to this day,

00:39:13.959 --> 00:39:16.807
I can go into rooms and say,
men still run the world, and

00:39:16.807 --> 00:39:19.120
people are like yes they do,
what a shocker.

00:39:19.120 --> 00:39:24.850
You're like, guys you have 95% of the top
shops this isn't that hard, right?

00:39:24.850 --> 00:39:28.810
But the answer is why, like what are the
cultural things that are holding us back?

00:39:28.810 --> 00:39:30.690
Ready men in the audience, men only.

00:39:30.690 --> 00:39:32.475
Raise you hand if anyone
ever called you bossy.

00:39:32.475 --> 00:39:38.810
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There's always one or two.

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:41.740
Women in the audience, raise your
hand if anyone ever called you bossy.

00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:46.650
I wanted her to turn around and
look at that, put your hands back up.

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:52.600
So next time you see a little
girl called bossy, and

00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:55.190
it will be this weekend if you look for
her, you walk right up to the people

00:39:55.190 --> 00:39:58.830
who called her bossy,
probably her parents, and you say,

00:39:58.830 --> 00:40:03.705
that little girl is not bossy, that little
girl has executive leadership skills.

00:40:03.705 --> 00:40:07.207
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [LAUGH]

00:40:07.207 --> 00:40:11.715
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:11.715 --> 00:40:14.270
&gt;&gt; Okay, I want you to wait, ready?

00:40:14.270 --> 00:40:16.449
I'm going to try that the other way.

00:40:16.449 --> 00:40:21.073
That little boy has
executive leadership skills.

00:40:21.073 --> 00:40:26.813
I have done that, exactly that way, and
gotten a huge laugh out of every audience,

00:40:26.813 --> 00:40:29.690
in every country I have ever done that in.

00:40:29.690 --> 00:40:32.582
But here's the bad news.

00:40:32.582 --> 00:40:38.280
That's bias, and if you don't think
you have it, I just proved you do.

00:40:38.280 --> 00:40:41.746
Because the reason we laugh is that it's
funny that a little girl has executive

00:40:41.746 --> 00:40:45.018
leadership skills, because it goes
against our expectations and type.

00:40:45.018 --> 00:40:49.084
So in case you sit here at
the Stanford Business School,

00:40:49.084 --> 00:40:54.984
thinking that other people have the biases
that hold women back, it's you too.

00:40:54.984 --> 00:41:01.048
I laughed, you laughed,
it's me and it's you.

00:41:01.048 --> 00:41:05.980
We all have the stereotype that
is keeping women from leading.

00:41:05.980 --> 00:41:09.920
And you just got through two years
at Stanford and it didn't change.

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:15.134
No, let's be honest,
that's what I wanted to say.

00:41:15.134 --> 00:41:19.058
And then I wanted to say there's a whole
bunch of stuff women are also doing to

00:41:19.058 --> 00:41:20.293
hold themselves back.

00:41:20.293 --> 00:41:25.156
So I don't think I chose to be any kind of
leader, I chose to say those three things.

00:41:25.156 --> 00:41:29.081
We need more women in leadership,
the culture is holding us back, and

00:41:29.081 --> 00:41:30.622
there are things we can do.

00:41:30.622 --> 00:41:34.208
And again I think, if you're looking for
what kind of leader you're going to be,

00:41:34.208 --> 00:41:35.610
you are too inwardly focused.

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:41.460
Focus on what you want to say, because it
matters, or what you want to build, or

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:43.640
what you want to make, or
who you want to serve.

00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:50.202
And that's how you become the kind of
leader you are, if that makes sense.

00:41:56.887 --> 00:41:58.417
&gt;&gt; I'm Paul, I'm a second year student,

00:41:58.417 --> 00:42:00.925
and I'm not trying to ask you if
you're going to run for president.

00:42:00.925 --> 00:42:04.340
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Which means that I am asking you that,

00:42:04.340 --> 00:42:07.090
but let's just frame it as career advice.

00:42:07.090 --> 00:42:09.280
So you worked in the Clinton
administration and

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:13.060
you're obviously very interested in
public service and the public sector.

00:42:13.060 --> 00:42:17.255
And for those of us who are interested in
both government and private sector work.

00:42:17.255 --> 00:42:21.473
How do you think about the pros and
cons and going forward in your career,

00:42:21.473 --> 00:42:23.769
how do you think about blending those?

00:42:23.769 --> 00:42:27.808
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's a business school question!

00:42:27.808 --> 00:42:28.593
&gt;&gt; That's a good question.

00:42:28.593 --> 00:42:32.219
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So for me, for my answer,

00:42:32.219 --> 00:42:36.900
is I really love my job at Facebook and
it gives me what I want to do.

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:42.880
And then, but I do have a loud voice, an
increasingly loud voice on public policy.

00:42:42.880 --> 00:42:44.795
I know what I think a whole
bunch of policies work and

00:42:44.795 --> 00:42:46.232
I'm increasingly using that voice.

00:42:46.232 --> 00:42:53.687
And I think I have the ability to have
that voice, and it's one I'm using loudly.

00:42:53.687 --> 00:42:56.041
Going in and out, look,
there's two paths in the US government.

00:42:56.041 --> 00:42:59.860
There's civil servants, where you really
go in and you move up the ladder, and

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:01.181
there's going in and out.

00:43:01.181 --> 00:43:04.263
I think being open to doing that,
thinking about running for office,

00:43:04.263 --> 00:43:05.949
all of that stuff really makes sense.

00:43:07.240 --> 00:43:09.920
And I think we need the country's best and
brightest to do this.

00:43:09.920 --> 00:43:12.520
I also think we'd be well
served by having more

00:43:12.520 --> 00:43:14.400
people who were in business in office.

00:43:15.510 --> 00:43:18.236
I think about some of the things
that I thought at the U.S.

00:43:18.236 --> 00:43:22.780
Treasury that really changed once I had to
make payroll, and that was the Treasury,

00:43:22.780 --> 00:43:24.130
which is the finance department.

00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:27.710
And so I do think having more business
leaders, like all of you, running for

00:43:27.710 --> 00:43:30.020
office and
serving in government is really important.

00:43:30.020 --> 00:43:33.990
Bob Rubin, who is the chairman of
Goldman Sachs and then the secretary of

00:43:33.990 --> 00:43:38.850
the treasury, made that point for a very
long time, and I think it's a good one.

00:43:38.850 --> 00:43:42.454
So I'm glad to hear you ask that question,
and I hope a lot of you are open to that,

00:43:42.454 --> 00:43:44.003
because you could do a lot of good.

00:43:47.017 --> 00:43:52.317
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:43:52.317 --> 00:43:54.625
&gt;&gt; A serious answer to the question is,

00:43:54.625 --> 00:43:57.751
I feel like I'm trying to
do all the good I can, and

00:43:57.751 --> 00:44:00.366
there's always different ways to do it.

00:44:00.366 --> 00:44:03.608
But I really believe in what
Facebook's doing, I really do.

00:44:03.608 --> 00:44:06.190
I believe that we need global companies,
I believe that we need global voice.

00:44:07.830 --> 00:44:11.797
I believe there are 4 billion people who
aren't connected to data that we can help

00:44:11.797 --> 00:44:14.624
connect, but it's because I believe that,
that I stay.

00:44:19.803 --> 00:44:22.645
&gt;&gt; My name is Kristof Meyer,
I'm a second year here.

00:44:22.645 --> 00:44:26.515
As COO of Facebook, you led
the company through tremendous growth.

00:44:26.515 --> 00:44:30.662
There's another rocket ship that's
facing a few problems right now,

00:44:30.662 --> 00:44:32.816
that I hear is also looking for a COO.

00:44:32.816 --> 00:44:37.334
What would you do if you
were the new COO of Uber?

00:44:37.334 --> 00:44:42.803
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean,

00:44:42.803 --> 00:44:46.258
it's a really hard question
to answer from the outside.

00:44:46.258 --> 00:44:48.917
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's a really hard question from

00:44:48.917 --> 00:44:52.540
the outside, I don't understand their
business deeply enough, I mean, at all,

00:44:52.540 --> 00:44:54.584
at all to answer, but
here's what I would do.

00:44:54.584 --> 00:44:57.358
I would take that job if I had
the relationship with Travis,

00:44:57.358 --> 00:44:59.700
that could really make an impact.

00:44:59.700 --> 00:45:02.820
And I felt there was a real desire to

00:45:02.820 --> 00:45:04.850
address the issues that
needed to be addressed.

00:45:04.850 --> 00:45:07.250
And it's not just the COO job.

00:45:07.250 --> 00:45:08.010
It really is,

00:45:08.010 --> 00:45:12.040
as you take every role you're going to
take, and you guys, I'm so honored and

00:45:12.040 --> 00:45:15.650
excited to be here as your, I guess
your last speaker before you graduate.

00:45:15.650 --> 00:45:19.596
Because how exciting, you've studied
at one of the best institutions,

00:45:19.596 --> 00:45:22.343
you have these tremendous
classmates around you.

00:45:22.343 --> 00:45:24.350
You're asking all of
these amazing questions.

00:45:24.350 --> 00:45:27.250
Like, go out and make a difference.

00:45:27.250 --> 00:45:31.250
And you're going to do that by finding
things you really care about, and

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:35.300
building relationships with the people
around you that make you care.

00:45:35.300 --> 00:45:38.390
I say at Facebook to this question, it
does get asked with some frequency, right?

00:45:38.390 --> 00:45:39.318
It's been nine and

00:45:39.318 --> 00:45:42.977
a half years because I'm doing something
I care about with my best friend.

00:45:42.977 --> 00:45:45.066
That's a pretty special thing to do.

00:45:45.066 --> 00:45:48.480
So whoever takes that job, if someone
takes that job, they have to believe in

00:45:48.480 --> 00:45:52.120
what Uber's doing, and they have to
believe in their relationship with Travis.

00:45:52.120 --> 00:45:54.744
And then they have to build
the team around them,

00:45:54.744 --> 00:45:57.181
and it's the exact same thing for
your jobs.

00:45:57.181 --> 00:46:00.027
Whatever you're about to go do,
if you haven't figured it out or

00:46:00.027 --> 00:46:01.130
you are figuring it out.

00:46:01.130 --> 00:46:05.306
Do something you care about
with people you believe and

00:46:05.306 --> 00:46:08.681
focus on what you contribute, that's it.

00:46:08.681 --> 00:46:12.641
And be willing to move around, I wrote in
Lean In that careers are a jungle gym,

00:46:12.641 --> 00:46:13.370
not a ladder.

00:46:13.370 --> 00:46:15.240
Careers are a jungle gym.

00:46:15.240 --> 00:46:18.120
I moved sideways, I didn't always move up.

00:46:18.120 --> 00:46:19.320
If I had insisted on moving up,

00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:22.380
I never would have taken
the number two job at Facebook.

00:46:22.380 --> 00:46:23.250
I could have been CEO,

00:46:23.250 --> 00:46:26.360
everything else that we're talking
about I would have been CEO.

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:28.390
If I took another job now,
I would run the company.

00:46:28.390 --> 00:46:33.761
But, I'd rather be number two at Facebook,
if that's even a concept, right?

00:46:33.761 --> 00:46:36.132
I mean, we have a team,
it's not just me and Mark,

00:46:36.132 --> 00:46:38.570
than be number one on
something I don't believe in.

00:46:38.570 --> 00:46:42.210
And I think the number two and the number
one is the wrong way to think about it.

00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:45.559
It's the relationship, the purpose,
the mission and what you can contribute.

00:46:46.690 --> 00:46:52.639
Same thing, your job decision is the same
as whoever's going to go be CEO of Uber.

00:46:52.639 --> 00:46:56.290
&gt;&gt; We have time for one final question.

00:46:56.290 --> 00:46:59.074
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Josh and
I'm a first year MBA student.

00:46:59.074 --> 00:47:00.418
And kind of following on to that,

00:47:00.418 --> 00:47:03.830
you mentioned how you navigated
these different career decisions?

00:47:03.830 --> 00:47:06.998
And so with getting to these rocket ships,

00:47:06.998 --> 00:47:12.551
what really made those companies and
teams that you've joined stand out?

00:47:12.551 --> 00:47:13.777
&gt;&gt; Well I've only done it twice, right?

00:47:13.777 --> 00:47:16.136
I did Google and Facebook.

00:47:16.136 --> 00:47:21.620
With Google, I really believed in a
mission of making the world's information,

00:47:21.620 --> 00:47:23.774
giving the world information.

00:47:23.774 --> 00:47:27.349
And I could see how much easier it was,
I mean when Google started, you know,

00:47:27.349 --> 00:47:28.619
I used to go to the library.

00:47:28.619 --> 00:47:31.014
Like, go to the library.

00:47:31.014 --> 00:47:32.881
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Literally,

00:47:32.881 --> 00:47:36.446
I grew up in Encyclopedia Britannica
in my dad's little study.

00:47:36.446 --> 00:47:43.040
It was just spell bounding how you could
just get information on your computer.

00:47:43.040 --> 00:47:44.540
I really believe there was an unmet need,
and

00:47:44.540 --> 00:47:47.590
with Facebook I felt like it turned
the lights on on who people were.

00:47:48.890 --> 00:47:51.030
Before Facebook, they had that,
you probably don't remember this,

00:47:51.030 --> 00:47:53.700
there was the famous cartoon of a dog
sitting in front of the computer.

00:47:53.700 --> 00:47:56.278
And it said, on the Internet,
no one knows you're a dog.

00:47:56.278 --> 00:47:58.436
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean Facebook was the first time

00:47:58.436 --> 00:48:00.870
there was real identity on the Internet.

00:48:00.870 --> 00:48:04.815
So everything I believe about
being authentic and who you are,

00:48:04.815 --> 00:48:09.295
was going to become true because of
this company run by this 23 year old.

00:48:09.295 --> 00:48:10.911
It was pretty amazing, and so

00:48:10.911 --> 00:48:14.090
it was really that I thought
they were making these needs.

00:48:14.090 --> 00:48:19.240
The other think I'll say is it's not,
along with passion, I do think it's really

00:48:19.240 --> 00:48:23.960
worth investing in your own skills, and
trying the things you're not as good at.

00:48:23.960 --> 00:48:27.633
Try the things that matter,
take a sales job.

00:48:27.633 --> 00:48:30.504
I think too few people out of
business will take sales jobs.

00:48:30.504 --> 00:48:35.772
I sell all day, I sell ads for
Facebook, I do BD deals where I sell.

00:48:35.772 --> 00:48:38.081
Every time I'm recruiting
someone I'm selling,

00:48:38.081 --> 00:48:40.506
when I'm trying to convince people,
we all do that.

00:48:40.506 --> 00:48:43.390
Take those jobs where you're
really rolling your hands up.

00:48:43.390 --> 00:48:46.560
I have a poster in my office
that I got from Howard Schultz,

00:48:46.560 --> 00:48:48.850
I served on his board.

00:48:48.850 --> 00:48:52.480
Of two dirty hands, and it says,
the future belongs to those

00:48:52.480 --> 00:48:56.940
of us still willing to get our hands
dirty, and it's in my conference room.

00:48:58.540 --> 00:49:02.418
Do something you care about and
get your hands dirty doing it.

00:49:02.418 --> 00:49:05.078
You'll be able to do anything, I promise.

00:49:05.078 --> 00:49:06.794
&gt;&gt; Sheryl, thank you so
much for being here.

00:49:06.794 --> 00:49:07.532
&gt;&gt; I want to say good luck!

00:49:07.532 --> 00:49:16.918
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:49:16.918 --> 00:49:20.590
[MUSIC]

